    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Pete Crow-Armstrong** serving as our clear offensive anchor. His score of 82.13 is the highest on the roster, driven by an ideal matchup against Griffin Canning. The combination of an elevated 4.60 xERA from the opposition, a favorable wind direction, and the high-leverage leadoff spot makes **Pete Crow-Armstrong** an elite play for stolen base production\u2014our scarcest category. Joining him at the top of the board is **Yordan Alvarez** (74.98), whose elite xwOBA of 0.444 against Matthews effectively ignores the neutral park factors. **Caleb Durbin** also remains a strong start at 67.44, as he takes advantage of a favorable 4.66 xERA from Miles Mikolas to provide stable efficiency across our counting stats.\n\nOur primary bench dilemma centered on **Luke Raley**, who posted a marginal 51.60. Despite the positive SP skill matchup against Ryan Johnson, **Luke Raley** was ultimately edged out of the starting lineup due to a bottom-third batting order projection and unfavorable park factors that neutralized his ceiling. Our decision-making process continues to prioritize elite sprint speed for stolen base volume, keeping players like **Carson Benge** (61.08) in the starting lineup to maximize leadoff opportunities, even when their base projections are tight.\n\nA note of caution regarding our lineup construction: several spots, including **Gary Sanchez**, **Casey Schmitt**, and **Christian Yelich**, are currently tracking against \"Lineup Pending\" status. While our projections account for historical tendencies, any significant shuffle in the batting order\u2014specifically for **Christian Yelich**\u2014could dampen his output against Nick Lodolo. Furthermore, we are keeping a close eye on **Trea Turner** and **Kyle Schwarber**; while they possess strong upside, they are facing Braxton Ashcraft, whose 3.37 xERA acts as the toughest hurdle in today's rotation. We remain committed to these veterans, but their lower-than-average SP skill scores suggest volatility if the opposing bullpen proves efficient.",
